{
 "id": "2026-ma-indoor",
 "name": "Indoor Greenery: Preference, Relaxation and Restorativeness",
 "description": "Method replication: same task, scales and design as the paper; the images are not the paper's stimuli (20 openly licensed Wikimedia Commons photographs of plants in indoor public spaces such as atriums, lobbies, malls and conservatories; the photographer and licence are shown under each photo). After background questions (gender, birth cohort, professional background, contact with plants) and the Nature Relatedness short form (NR-6), participants see one group of 20 photographs in a fixed order and rate each for preference (\"How much do you like this greenery scene?\") and relaxation (\"How relaxed do you feel while looking at this image?\") on 1–5. Then they answer the I-PANAS-SF, an 11-item Perceived Restorativeness Scale about \"places like these\" and three behavioural-intention items. The paper links plant visual features to the image ratings with linear mixed models and, at group level, relates cumulative restorative experience to affect, perceived restorativeness and intention with PLS-SEM; Green View Index and species richness were the most stable positive features. Differences from the original study: (1) one group of 20 photographs instead of 200 images in 10 groups; add more groups by uploading files named g02_01.jpg … g02_20.jpg and each participant gets one group at random; (2) the paper's plant feature coding (GVI, species richness, health, planting mode) is not included. Cite: Ma, Hou, Chen, Zhu, Chen & Wilkinson (2026), Land 15(5): 741.",
